course: dictionary
lesson: 3884
title: Freezing: cold, ice or stop still
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the clues around it show you which one you need. «Thunderstorm clouds reach so high that their tops are far below freezing, even in summer.» Far below freezing means colder than the temperature where water turns to ice, so freezing is a temperature here.
explain: «Kai earned his gift card on a freezing Sunday, selling old toys at a car boot sale.» The word describes the day, so freezing means very cold. Test it: swap in very cold and the sentence still makes sense.
explain: «Deer often freeze in bright headlights.» Freeze is a verb here. The sudden light is the clue: the deer suddenly stop still, because they are frightened. Check the verb forms too: freezes, froze and frozen are all from the same word.
fact: Freezing can mean very cold, as in «I'm freezing!»
fact: Water freezes at 0 °C (32 °F). Freezing point is the temperature at which a liquid becomes solid.
fact: Freezing is the opposite of melting, and it is a change you can reverse: ice melts back into water when it warms up.
fact: When a deer or a person freezes, they suddenly stop still, often because they are scared.
fact: Other phrases to learn are freezing cold, below freezing and freezing fog.
exercise: «Kai earned his gift card on a freezing Sunday.» What does freezing mean here? | Very hot | Very quiet | Very late | *Very cold
exercise: «Deer often freeze in bright headlights.» What does freeze mean here? | Melt slowly | *Suddenly stop still, often from fear | Turn into a cloud | Run away fast
exercise: What is freezing point? | *The temperature at which a liquid becomes solid | A cold drink with lots of ice | The hottest time of the day
test: What is the opposite of freezing, when ice warms up? | Drizzling | Shivering | *Melting | Blowing
test: «Far below freezing» tells you the temperature is what? | It is warmer than a summer day | *It is colder than 0 degrees Celsius | It is exactly 10 degrees | It is as warm as a bath
exam: Which sentence uses freezing to mean very cold? | *«I'm freezing!» said Kai, pulling on his gloves | Water in the pond began to freeze | The ice melted in the sun | The deer stood frozen in the headlights
